+# Recipes — the measured configuration per model
+
+The feature scoreboard in [host-benchmarks.md](host-benchmarks.md) answers "which feature
+wins"; this doc answers "what should I actually run". One section per model: the
+recommended flag set, where each flag comes from, what it measured on that model, and the
+total speedup against the plain-mmap baseline that any llama.cpp-based runtime starts
+from. Every number is copied from [host-benchmarks.md](host-benchmarks.md) (host: 4-core
+x86-64, 11 GB RAM, SSD — [method](benchmark-method.md)); nothing here is measured
+anywhere else, so the two docs must move together.
+
+**Provenance legend:**
+
+- **bmoe-main** — ships in Helldez's BigMoeOnEdge (`origin/main`): the streaming engine,
+  its compute/io knobs, and the expert-ready seam commit in the submodule. This fork
+  builds on that engine; these are not the fork's to claim.
+- **fork (this repo)** — added by the `cjl4hd` fork: the session-residency layer
+  (`--rs-seq`, the serve bridge, warmup replay, `--auto-echo`) and seam PR #29085.
+- **mainline (llama.cpp)** — inherited from llama.cpp (e.g. ubatch; the ngram-mod
+  drafting stack, upstream PR #19164).
+- **refuted / superseded** — implemented, measured, and rejected; kept per
+  [ADR-005](adr/005-keep-off-features-policy.md). Provenance for these is bmoe-main's
+  unless marked fork.
+
+Each model's per-flag table is collapsed; open it for the flag-by-flag measurements and
+verdicts. The models are grouped by memory regime, because the regime — not the
+architecture — decides which layer pays.
+
+## Contents
+
+**Past RAM (~2× host RAM) — bmoe-main's streaming engine pays:**
+
+- [Ornith 1.5-35B](#ornith-15-35b-qwen35moe-hybrid-202-gb--2-host-ram)
+- [Cyber-Tiel-Coder-35B-A3B](#cyber-tiel-coder-35b-a3b-qwen35moe-hybrid-coder-210-gb--2-host-ram)
+- [Qwen3.6-35B-A3B](#qwen36-35b-a3b-qwen35moe-hybrid-221-gb--2-host-ram)
+- [Qwen3-30B-A3B](#qwen3-30b-a3b-qwen3moe-plain-moe-186-gb--2-host-ram)
+- [Laguna-XS-2.1](#laguna-xs-21-laguna-plain-moe-189-gb--2-host-ram)
+
+**Fits RAM — the stack is off; the fork's session layer is the value:**
+
+- [Ling-mini-2.0](#ling-mini-20-bailingmoe2-plain-moe-99-gb--barely-fits-host-ram)
+- [LFM2.5-8B-A1B](#lfm25-8b-a1b-lfm2moe-hybrid-50-gb--fits-host-ram-comfortably)
+- [OLMoE-1B-7B](#olmoe-1b-7b-olmoe-plain-moe-40-gb--fits-host-ram-comfortably)
+
+The pattern all eight establish: **past-RAM models buy bmoe-main's streaming + substitution
+(+42–70% streaming decode, +47% substitution on the best cell); fits-RAM models leave the
+stack off (−6% to −31%) and take the fork's session layer (warmup ~10–56×, follow-up
+prefill ~4–6× lower, divergence rewind 2.2–2.3× where it engages).** Model size decides
+which layer pays.
+
+## Past-RAM models — bmoe-main's decode, the fork's turn latency
+
+Three `qwen35moe` hybrids and two plain-transformer MoEs at ~2× host RAM. The mmap
+baseline thrashes (129–642 majflt/tok); the streaming stack reads instead and wins
++42–70% decode, and on the plain MoEs prefill speeds up too. The fork's session layer
+then owns prefill and turn latency; its decode contribution is 0% by design.
+
+### Ornith 1.5-35B (`qwen35moe` hybrid, 20.2 GB, ~2× host RAM)
+
+**Recommended recipe (single-shot / cold sessions):**
+
+```bash
+bmoe-cli -m Ornith-1.5-35B-Q4_K_M.gguf \
+  --moe-stream --cache-mb auto --io-threads 4 --overlap --dense-weights anon
+```
+
+**Add for served / multi-turn sessions:** warmup replay + `--auto-echo` (the bridge).
+`--rs-seq 64` is eligible on the bench build — its divergence rewind engaged (33 prompt /
+23 reused, answer verified) but on this IO-bound host it proved mechanism, not latency:
+the rewind's wall-clock win needs a host where skipped tokens are worth skipping (see
+Cyber-Tiel). `--auto-echo` echo-reuse cannot engage on the qwen35 template (empty `<think>`
+baked into re-rendered history) until the first echoed turn reconciles.
+
+<details>
+<summary><strong>Per-flag table (provenance marked) — click to expand</strong></summary>
+
+| Flag | Provenance | Measured on this model | Verdict |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| streaming stack (a→b) | **bmoe-main** | **+48%** (1.39 → 2.06 tok/s), majflt/tok 470 → 129 | **On** — the base for past-RAM models |
+| `--ubatch 512` | **mainline** | protocol default | **On** (protocol) |
+| warmup replay (bridge) | **fork** | T1 prefill 40.7 → **8.6 s (4.7×)** — the largest absolute warmup in the matrix (32 s cut) | **On** for served use |
+| `--auto-echo` (bridge) | **fork** | T3 prefill −50% (28 prompt / 98 reused), T3 decode **+69%**; T2 stays a full clear (first echoed turn reconciles) | **On** for served use |
+| `--rs-seq 64` | **fork** (seam PR #29085) | divergence rewind ENGAGED (T2 33 prompt / 23 reused, 62 correct, no degeneration) — but prefill ≈ unchanged (15.5 vs 14.5 s): IO-bound host, cached-token compute is not the bottleneck | **On** for served use; latency win is host-dependent |
+| `--mtp` | **bmoe-main** (mainline concept) | not measured on this model | candidate cell |
+| `--expert-substitute` · `--drop-cold-experts` | **bmoe-main** | not measured on this model (+47.2% / +32.3% on Cyber-Tiel, same family) | Use-when: thrash (by family evidence) |
+
+</details>
+
+**Totals (two metrics).** (1) bmoe vs llama: baseline 1.39 → **2.06 tok/s (+48%)** with
+faults collapsing 470 → 129 — all bmoe-main. (2) fork vs bmoe-main: warmup 4.7×, T3
+prefill halved with auto-echo, divergence rewind proven live (the index-shift fix's first
+end-to-end proof through the real engine); decode contribution 0% by design.
+
+### Cyber-Tiel-Coder-35B-A3B (`qwen35moe` hybrid coder, 21.0 GB, ~2× host RAM)
+
+The deepest-thrash coder cell (~18% routing miss headroom) and the substitution + MTP
+carrier. The engine's headline numbers live here.
+
+**Recommended recipe (single-shot / cold sessions):**
+
+```bash
+bmoe-cli -m Cyber-Tiel-Coder-35B-A3B-MTP-UD-Q4_K_M.gguf \
+  --moe-stream --cache-mb auto --io-threads 4 --overlap --dense-weights anon \
+  --expert-substitute 0.15
